\setlength{\baselineskip}{19bp}
\noindent \uline{Proof}.\quad By Lazard--Deligne (I 4.2.1.3), $E$ is the local inductive limit of a local\par
\noindent inductive $T$-system of free modules. Since local inductive limits are exact (loc. cit.),\par
\noindent by (1.2.3.4) we may therefore suppose that $E=A^{(X)}$ for some $X\in\operatorname{ob}T$. We\par
\noindent then have $B\xrightarrow{\sim}A[X]$. Applying (I 1.5.3 (ii)) to the functors $T=A[-]$ and\par
\noindent $U=$ ``forgetful'', the augmentation $P_A(A[X])\longrightarrow A[X]$ is a homotopy equivalence (of\par
\noindent simplicial $A$-algebras). Hence $\Omega^1_{P/A}\longrightarrow\Omega^1_{B/A}$ (where $P=P_A(B)$) is a homotopy\par
\noindent equivalence of simplicial $A$-modules, and thus in particular a quasi-isomorphism. Since the\par
\noindent components of $P$ are free, $\Omega^1_{P/A}$ is, by (1.1.3.2), a flat $P$-module. It follows from\par
\noindent (I 3.3.4.6) that $\Omega^1_{P/A}\otimes_PB\longrightarrow\Omega^1_{B/A}$ is a quasi-isomorphism, qed.

