\phantomsection\hypertarget{illusie:I:ch1:p143:corollary-2.3.1.1}{}%
\noindent \uline{Corollary} 2.3.1.1.\quad \uline{Let}

\vspace{5bp}
\phantomsection\hypertarget{illusie:I:ch1:p143:ring-square-2.3.1.1}{}%
\noindent\hspace{130.5bp}\IllusieLocalizationCorollarySquarePOneFortyThree\par

\vspace{5bp}
\phantomsection\hypertarget{illusie:I:ch1:p143:horizontal-epimorphism-flatness-hypotheses}{}%
\noindent \uline{be a commutative square of rings on}\quad $T$. \uline{Suppose that the horizontal arrows are}\par
\noindent \uline{epimorphisms in the category of rings and that}\quad $A'$ (resp. $B'$) \uline{is flat}\par
\noindent \uline{over}\quad $A$ (resp. $B$). \uline{Then the canonical map}\quad
\phantomsection\hypertarget{illusie:I:ch1:p143:cotangent-base-change-quasi-isomorphism}{}%
$L_{B/A}\otimes_B B'\longrightarrow L_{B'/A'}$ \uline{is a}\par
\noindent \uline{quasi-isomorphism}.

\vspace{5bp}
\phantomsection\hypertarget{illusie:I:ch1:p143:proof-2.3.1.1}{}%
\noindent \uline{Proof}. By (2.3.1),
\phantomsection\hypertarget{illusie:I:ch1:p143:localization-cotangent-vanishings}{}%
$L_{A'/A}$ (resp. $L_{B'/B}$) is zero in $D(A')$ (resp. $D(B'))$.\par
\phantomsection\hypertarget{illusie:I:ch1:p143:transitivity-triangle-conclusion}{}%
\noindent The assertion therefore follows from the transitivity triangles associated with $A\longrightarrow B\longrightarrow B'$\par
\noindent and $A\longrightarrow A'\longrightarrow B'$.

\vspace{5bp}
\phantomsection\hypertarget{illusie:I:ch1:p143:criterion-2.3.2}{}%
\noindent 2.3.2.\quad The hypotheses of (2.3.1) are satisfied in particular if $u$ is a\par
\phantomsection\hypertarget{illusie:I:ch1:p143:localization-morphism-definition}{}%
\noindent \uline{localization morphism}, by which we mean that there exists a subsheaf of sets\par
\noindent $X$ of $A$ such that $u(X)$ is contained in the subsheaf $B^*$ of units of $B$,\par
\noindent and that the canonical map $X^{-1}A\longrightarrow B$ induced by $u$ is an isomorphism\par
\noindent (cf. ([17] 9.4.4)). \phantomsection\hypertarget{illusie:I:ch1:p143:fraction-ring-universal-property}{}%
(Recall that the ring of fractions $X^{-1}A$ and\par
\noindent the canonical map $A\longrightarrow X^{-1}A$ are characterized by the usual universal property,\par
\noindent namely that every map from $A$ to a ring $A'$ carrying $X$ into $A'{}^*$ factors uniquely\par
\noindent through $X^{-1}A$.) \phantomsection\hypertarget{illusie:I:ch1:p143:maximal-localizing-subsheaf}{}%
Note that if $u$ is a localization morphism,\par
\vspace{4.7bp}
\noindent there is a largest $X$ having the property indicated above, namely $u^{-1}(B^*)$.\par
\noindent That a localization morphism
\phantomsection\hypertarget{illusie:I:ch1:p143:localization-epimorphism-flatness}{}%
is an epimorphism in the category of rings is immediate from\par
\vspace{1.5bp}
\noindent the definition; as for the fact that $X^{-1}A$ is flat over $A$, it is verified easily just as in\par
\vspace{1.5bp}
\noindent the case of ordinary rings.

