\phantomsection\hypertarget{illusie:I:ch1:p147:proof-2.3.7}{}%
\noindent \uline{Proof}. Since the question is local by (2.3.0), we may suppose that $X$ and $Y$ are affine.\par
\noindent The first assertion then follows immediately from (2.3.6.3). The second also follows,\par
\noindent in view of (1.2.6.3) and the fact that, by André ([1] 17.2 p.69), every finite-type\par
\phantomsection\hypertarget{illusie:I:ch1:p147:andre-finite-free-resolution}{}%
\noindent algebra $B$ over a Noetherian ring $A$ admits a resolution $P\longrightarrow B$ in which $P$ is a free\par
\noindent simplicial $A$-algebra of finite type in each degree.

\vspace{5bp}
\phantomsection\hypertarget{illusie:I:ch1:p147:proposition-2.3.8}{}%
\noindent \uline{Proposition} 2.3.8.\quad \uline{Let}\quad $S'\longrightarrow S$ \uline{be a morphism of ringed topoi},\quad $X$ \uline{a scheme}\par
\noindent \uline{relative to}\quad $S$ ([18]chap. IV), and let $X'$ \uline{be the relative scheme over}\quad $S'$ \uline{obtained by base}\par
\noindent \uline{change}, \uline{whence a commutative square}\textsuperscript{(1)} \uline{of ringed topoi}

\vspace{0.6bp}
\phantomsection\hypertarget{illusie:I:ch1:p147:base-change-square-2.3.8}{}%
\begin{center}\hspace*{-32.8bp}\IllusieRingedToposBaseChangeSquarePOneFortySeven\end{center}


