\noindent\hspace*{167.0bp}\IllusieToposMorphismPOneSixtyOne\par

\vspace{5bp}
\phantomsection\hypertarget{ILL-CCD:LNM239:CH3:S1:1:10:def}{}%
be a morphism of ringed topoi. By a \uline{first-order infinitesimal $Y$-neighborhood} (or $Y$ 
\uline{extension}) of $X$ we mean a commutative diagram\textsuperscript{(1)} of ringed topoi\par


\vspace{5bp}
\phantomsection\hypertarget{ILL-CCD:LNM239:CH3:S1:1:10:eq1}{}%
\noindent\hspace*{7.0bp}\IllusieInfinitesimalNeighborhoodTrianglePOneSixtyOne\par

\vspace{5bp}
\phantomsection\hypertarget{ILL-CCD:LNM239:CH3:S1:1:10:eq1:condition}{}%
where $i$ is an equivalence on the underlying topoi and $i^{-1}(\underline{\mathrm{O}}_{X'})\longrightarrow\underline{\mathrm{O}}_X$ is 
\phantomsection\hypertarget{ILL-CCD:LNM239:CH3:S1:1:10:morphism:lead}{}%
surjective with square-zero kernel\textsuperscript{(2)}; a morphism of $Y$-extensions of $X$ is defined\par


\vspace{5bp}
\phantomsection\hypertarget{ILL-CCD:LNM239:CH3:S1:1:10:eq1:n1}{}%
\noindent\hspace*{-1.0bp}\rule{72.5bp}{0.4bp}\par
\ReflowBodyFont\IllusieUprightLatinMath
\textsuperscript{(1)} up to a specified isomorphism, as in (II 1.1.4), an isomorphism most often\par

\noindent\hspace*{-0.5bp}{suppressed in practice.}\par
\phantomsection\hypertarget{ILL-CCD:LNM239:CH3:S1:1:10:eq1:n2}{}%
\textsuperscript{(2)} in practice, we shall often proceed as if the topoi underlying $X$ and $X'$ were\par

\noindent\hspace*{-0.5bp}{the same, with $i$ the identity on them.}\par

