\phantomsection\hypertarget{ILL-CCD:LNM239:CH3:S1:2:5:4:proof:resolution-notation}{}%
exact sequence (II 2.1.1.3)\par


\vspace{5bp}
\phantomsection\hypertarget{ILL-CCD:LNM239:CH3:S1:2:5:4:proof:exact-sequence:lead}{}%
\phantomsection\hypertarget{ILL-CCD:LNM239:CH3:S1:2:5:4:proof:exact-sequence}{}%
\noindent\makebox[0pt][l]{(*)}\hspace*{90.0bp}\IllusieCotangentExactSequencePOneSeventy
\par

\vspace{5bp}
\phantomsection\hypertarget{ILL-CCD:LNM239:CH3:S1:2:5:4:proof:base-change}{}%
which, by extension of scalars to $C$, gives triangle (1.2.5.2). This being so, 
the compatibility to be proved will follow from this one: $M$ now denoting a 
\phantomsection\hypertarget{ILL-CCD:LNM239:CH3:S1:2:5:4:proof:compatibility:lead}{}%
$Q$-module, the square below\par


\vspace{5bp}
\phantomsection\hypertarget{ILL-CCD:LNM239:CH3:S1:2:5:4:proof:compatibility:square}{}%
\noindent\hspace*{74.7bp}\IllusieBoundaryCompatibilitySquarePOneSeventy
\par

\vspace{5bp}
\phantomsection\hypertarget{ILL-CCD:LNM239:CH3:S1:2:5:4:proof:compatibility:identifications}{}%
where the vertical arrows are the canonical isomorphisms and the upper horizontal arrow 
is the boundary operator defined by the exact sequence (*), is commutative. By 
\phantomsection\hypertarget{ILL-CCD:LNM239:CH3:S1:2:5:4:proof:functorial-reduction}{}%
functoriality, we may reduce to assuming $M=\Omega^1_{P/A}\otimes_P Q$ and verifying that the 
images of the identity map of $\Omega^1_{P/A}\otimes_P Q$ under the two composites are equal, or, 
in other words, that one has the relation\par


\vspace{5bp}
