\phantomsection\hypertarget{ILL-CCD:LNM239:CH3:S2:section}{}
\ReflowDestination{rf-toc-035}{1}{2. Deformations of ringed topoi and morphisms of ringed topoi}
%
2.\quad \uline{Deformations of ringed topoi and morphisms of ringed topoi}.\par


\vspace{5bp}
\phantomsection\hypertarget{ILL-CCD:LNM239:CH3:S2:introduction}{}%
We return in this section to some deformation problems studied by Grothendieck 
in [17]. Having the “right” cotangent complex enables us to refine and complete his study,\par

\noindent especially concerning the “variation” of obstructions.
\par

\vspace{5bp}
\phantomsection\hypertarget{ILL-CCD:LNM239:CH3:S2:1:subsection}{}
\ReflowDestination{rf-toc-036}{2}{2.1. Deformations of ringed topoi}
%
\noindent 2.1.\quad \uline{Deformations of ringed topoi}.
\par

\vspace{5bp}
\phantomsection\hypertarget{ILL-CCD:LNM239:CH3:S2:1:1:setup}{}%
2.1.1.\quad We first fix a topos $T$ and a ring $A$ on $T$\hspace{3.0bp}. Recall that there is a\par

\vspace{1.68bp}
\phantomsection\hypertarget{ILL-CCD:LNM239:CH3:S2:1:1:functor}{}%
\noindent functor (1.1.1.2)
\par

\vspace{5bp}
\noindent\hspace*{112.2bp}\IllusieToposFunctorPOneEightyFour
\hspace*{35.0bp}\textsuperscript{(1)}
\par

\vspace{5bp}
\phantomsection\hypertarget{ILL-CCD:LNM239:CH3:S2:1:1:object-action}{}%
which associates to each $A$-extension $0\longrightarrow I\longrightarrow B\longrightarrow B_o$ the pair $(B_o,I)$\hspace{3.0bp}, and to\par

\vspace{1.56bp}
\phantomsection\hypertarget{ILL-CCD:LNM239:CH3:S2:1:1:morphism-action}{}%
\noindent each morphism of $A$-extensions
\par

\vspace{5bp}
\phantomsection\hypertarget{ILL-CCD:LNM239:CH3:S2:1:1:diagram}{}%
\noindent\hspace*{43.5bp}\IllusieToposExtensionDiagramPOneEightyFour
\par

\vspace{5bp}
\phantomsection\hypertarget{ILL-CCD:LNM239:CH3:S2:1:1:pi-f}{}%
the pair $\pi(f)=(f_o,u)$, where $u:I\longrightarrow J$ is the morphism of $B_o$-modules induced by $f$\hspace{3.0bp}.\par

\vspace{0.72bp}
\noindent Note that the morphism of $C_o$-modules
\par

\vspace{5bp}
\phantomsection\hypertarget{ILL-CCD:LNM239:CH3:S2:1:1:v}{}%
\noindent\hspace*{134.0bp}\IllusieToposAdjunctionMapPOneEightyFour
\par

\vspace{5bp}
\phantomsection\hypertarget{ILL-CCD:LNM239:CH3:S2:1:1:image}{}%
defined by $u$, by adjunction, has image $I.C$. Thus $v$ is surjective if and only if 
\phantomsection\hypertarget{ILL-CCD:LNM239:CH3:S2:1:1:pushout-criterion}{}%
the square of rings defined by \uline{$f$} is cocartesian. Recall also the familiar flatness criterion 
\phantomsection\hypertarget{ILL-CCD:LNM239:CH3:S2:1:1:flatness-criterion}{}%
(cf. (Bourbaki, Alg. Com., Chap. III, § 5, th. 1), whose proof extends trivially to rings\par

\noindent in a topos)\hspace{3.0bp}:
\par


\phantomsection\hypertarget{ILL-CCD:LNM239:CH3:S2:1:1:n1}{}%
\noindent\rule{69.7bp}{0.4bp}
\par
\ReflowBodyFont\IllusieUprightLatinMath
\textsuperscript{(1)} A-Algmod is the subcategory of Algmod (1.1.1) consisting of pairs $(B,M)$, where 
$B$ is an $A$-algebra and $M$ is a $B$-module; a morphism $(B,M)\longrightarrow(C,N)$ is the pair 
of a morphism of $A$-algebras $B\longrightarrow C$ and a morphism of $B$-modules $M\longrightarrow N$\hspace{3.0bp}.\par

